What is Views in SQL.
==========================
    Views in SQL (Simple Explanation)
    A view is a virtual table because it doesn’t store data directly like normal tables. It just stores a query. 
    When you use (execute) the view, it runs the query and shows data from the real tables.

Why views are useful:

    Simplify complex queries
        Instead of writing long queries with multiple tables every time, you can create a view once and just use it.
        Example: Instead of joining Students, Courses, and Enrollments every time, create a view called StudentCourseView and then do:
        SELECT * FROM StudentCourseView;

    **Hide complexity 
        Users don’t need to know how many tables exist or how they are connected. The view gives a simple interface to see data.

    **Improve security
        You can show only specific columns to users.
        Example: Show only student names and departments, hide phone numbers or salaries.

    **Reusability
        Once a view is created, you can use it in different reports or modules without writing the same query again.
        and we can also make extra join

    Consistency
        Everyone using the view sees the same data and logic, which reduces mistakes.

    Materialized views (optional) – Some views can store the result of the query. This makes heavy queries faster because 
    the data is precomputed.

    Updatable views – In some cases, you can insert, update, or delete data through a view. The changes go to the original table,
     not the view itself.

Summary:
    Views make complex queries simple
    Hide table structure from users
    Improve security by showing only required data
    Can be reused across reports
    Keep data consistent
    Sometimes improve performance
    We can also make joins on resulted view data

UseCases:
    **Used heavily in reporting and analytics.
    **Can be reused across multiple reports and dashboards screens.
    Create views to combine multiple tables (like Orders, Customers, Products) into one virtual table.
    Analysts and BI tools query the view instead of writing complex joins.

    In short: Views make working with databases easier, safer, and cleaner.